          "text": [
            "Making a classic **Chicken Dum Biryani** at home might seem intimidating, but if you break it down into steps, it is highly achievable and incredibly rewarding. \n",
            "\n",
            "Here is a step-by-step recipe for an authentic, restaurant-style **Chicken Dum Biryani** (serves 4-5).\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### **Ingredients**\n",
            "\n",
            "#### **1. For the Chicken Marinade:**\n",
            "*   **Chicken:** 1 kg (2.2 lbs), curry cut (preferably bone-in for more flavor)\n",
            "*   **Yogurt (Dahi):** 1 cup (thick yogurt)\n",
            "*   **Ginger-Garlic Paste:** 2 tablespoons\n",
            "*   **Red Chili Powder:** 1.5 teaspoons\n",
            "*   **Turmeric Powder:** 1/2 teaspoon\n",
            "*   **Biryani Masala Powder:** 2 tablespoons (store-bought or homemade)\n",
            "*   **Lemon Juice:** 1 tablespoon\n",
            "*   **Salt:** 1.5 teaspoons (to taste)\n",
            "*   **Fried Onions (Birista):** 1 cup (crushed)\n",
            "*   **Fresh Mint & Coriander (Cilantro):** 1/2 cup each, finely chopped\n",
            "*   **Oil:** 3 tablespoons (use the oil used for frying the onions)\n",
            "\n",
            "#### **2. For the Rice:**\n",
            "*   **Basmati Rice:** 3 cups (long-grain, soaked in water for 30 minutes)\n",
            "*   **Whole Spices:** 2 bay leaves, 1 cinnamon stick, 4 green cardamoms, 4 cloves, 1 star anise, 1 teaspoon shahi jeera (caraway seeds)\n",
            "*   **Salt:** 2.5 tablespoons (the water should taste as salty as seawater)\n",
            "*   **Water:** 4 liters (for boiling)\n",
            "*   **Vinegar or Lemon juice:** 1 teaspoon (keeps rice grains white and separate)\n",
            "\n",
            "#### **3. For Layering & Garnish:**\n",
            "*   **Saffron Strands:** A pinch, soaked in 1/4 cup of warm milk (or yellow food color)\n",
            "*   **Ghee (Clarified Butter):** 3 tablespoons\n",
            "*   **Fried Onions:** 1/2 cup\n",
            "*   **Fresh Mint & Coriander:** 1/4 cup, chopped\n",
            "*   **Rose water or Kewra water:** 1 teaspoon (optional, for aroma)\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### **Step-by-Step Instructions**\n",
            "\n",
            "#### **Step 1: Fry the Onions (Birista)**\n",
            "1. Slice 3 large onions very thinly.\n",
            "2. Heat oil in a pan and deep-fry the onions on medium heat until they are golden brown and crispy. \n",
            "3. Drain them on paper towels. *Do not let them turn dark brown, or they will taste bitter.*\n",
            "\n",
            "#### **Step 2: Marinate the Chicken**\n",
            "1. In a large bowl, mix the chicken with all the ingredients listed under \"For the Chicken Marinade\" (including half of the fried onions).\n",
            "2. Mix well, rubbing the spices into the chicken.\n",
            "3. Cover and marinate in the refrigerator for **at least 1 hour** (overnight is best).\n",
            "\n",
            "#### **Step 3: Cook the Rice (Crucial Step)**\n",
            "1. In a large pot, bring 4 liters of water to a rolling boil.\n",
            "2. Add the whole spices, salt, and vinegar/lemon juice.\n",
            "3. Drain the soaked Basmati rice and add it to the boiling water.\n",
            "4. Cook the rice for exactly **5 to 6 minutes**. The rice should be **70% cooked** (it should have a bite to it and not be fully soft).\n",
            "5. Drain the rice completely in a colander, leaving the whole spices in.\n",
            "\n",
            "#### **Step 4: Cook the Chicken Base**\n",
            "1. Transfer the marinated chicken into a heavy-bottomed pot (this will be your Biryani pot). \n",
            "2. Cook the chicken on medium-high heat for 7-8 minutes until it changes color and starts releasing its juices. *We don't want to cook it fully, just about 50%, as it will finish cooking on \"dum\".*\n",
            "\n",
            "#### **Step 5: Layering the Biryani**\n",
            "1. Turn the heat to the lowest setting under your chicken pot. Spread the chicken evenly at the bottom of the pot.\n",
            "2. Layer the cooked 70% rice evenly over the chicken.\n",
            "3. **Garnish layer:** \n",
            "   * Sprinkle the remaining fried onions, chopped mint, and coriander leaves over the rice.\n",
            "   * Drizzle the saffron milk and ghee evenly over the top.\n",
            "   * Sprinkle the Kewra/rose water if using.\n",
            "\n",
            "#### **Step 6: The \"Dum\" (Steam Cooking)**\n",
            "1. To trap the steam inside, seal the pot. You can do this by wrapping aluminium foil over the rim of the pot and then putting the lid on tightly, or by sealing the lid with a dough made of flour and water.\n",
            "2. **First 5 minutes:** Cook on **medium-high heat** to build up steam inside the pot.\n",
            "3. **Next 15-20 minutes:** Place a flat tawa (griddle) under the pot, turn the heat to **low**, and let it cook. (The tawa prevents the bottom of the Biryani from burning).\n",
            "4. Turn off the heat and let the pot sit, unopened, for **another 10 minutes** to allow the flavors to settle.\n",
            "\n",
            "---\n",
            "\n",
            "### **How to Serve**\n",
            "Gently open the lid. Using a flat spatula, scoop the Biryani from the side of the pot, ensuring you get both white rice, spiced rice, and a piece of chicken. \n",
            "\n",
            "Serve hot with **Raita** (yogurt dip) and sliced onions. Enjoy your homemade masterpiece!\n"
          ]
        }
